<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dorchester-on-Thames</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dorchester-on-Thames is a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxfordshire">Oxfordshire</a> on the River Thame near its confluence with the Thames. A Roman small town, it lay at the heart of early West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> settlement, and in 635 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birinus">Birinus</a> baptised King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cynegils">Cynegils</a> there and made it the seat of the first West Saxon bishopric. Later the seat of a Mercian bishopric until 1072, it keeps its large medieval abbey church.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dorchester-on-thames.jpg" alt="Dorchester-on-Thames" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roger Davies · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dorchester_Post_Office_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1419421.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
